    "description": "Iran's men's basketball team won Asian championship titles in 2007, 2009, and 2013, with center Hamed Haddadi central to that period. The women's national team follows a separate competitive pathway and reached the 2025 Women's Asia Cup Division B final.",
    "tags": ["Basketball", "Hamed Haddadi", "Asia Cup", "Women's sport", "National teams"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:03:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:03:55Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Basketball in Iran: Asian Titles and Women's Competition",
      "description": "Understand Iran's basketball achievements, Hamed Haddadi's role, and the distinct competitive development of the women's national team."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "continental-generation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The generation that changed Iran's Asian standing",
        "content": "Iran's modern men's basketball story has a clear continental turning point: its first Asian championship title in 2007. Further titles followed in 2009 and 2013. The international governing body, FIBA, identifies Hamed Haddadi as a central figure in that run, describing a center whose influence extended beyond scoring close to the basket. [s1]\n\nA center normally operates near the basket, where rebounding and interior defense are important responsibilities. Haddadi also created opportunities through passing: FIBA's retrospective emphasizes his ability to draw defenders and find teammates. That makes his career useful for understanding how a prominent player can shape a team's collective style. Iran later finished runner-up in the 2017 Asia Cup, when Haddadi again received the tournament's Most Valuable Player award. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recent-mens-result",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A later return to the podium",
        "content": "The men's team returned to the Asia Cup podium in 2025. The competition's official results record Iran defeating New Zealand in the third-place game. This bronze medal is a separate achievement from the earlier championship titles and provides a later reference point when following the national team's development. [s2]\n\nSuch results answer a specific question: how a selected national team performed against its tournament opponents. They do not directly measure the number of people playing basketball, the financial condition of domestic clubs, or access to courts. A continental medal is strong evidence of tournament success; it should not be used as a substitute for evidence about the entire basketball system. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "womens-pathway",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Women's competition and the promotion boundary",
        "content": "Women's basketball needs a distinct account. In 2025, Iran reached the Women's Asia Cup Division B final in Shenzhen, China. Chinese Taipei won that game and secured promotion to Division A. Iran's runner-up finish therefore belonged to Division B; describing it simply as second place in the Women's Asia Cup would obscure the competition's structure. [s3]\n\nFIBA's match report identifies Mahla Abedi as an important contributor in the final. It also notes that Iran had reached the Division B final in consecutive editions. This records competitive progress while making the remaining promotion barrier visible. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "headgear-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why equipment rules mattered",
        "content": "An important international rule change concerned headgear. FIBA announced that a revised rule permitting compliant head coverings would take effect on 1 October 2017. Its explanation explicitly connected the change to dress requirements that had conflicted with the previous rule and discussed an Iranian women's test game. This documents an eligibility-related change; it does not establish that all practical barriers to participation disappeared. [s4]\n\nTogether, the men's championship record and women's promotion campaigns show why Iranian basketball is best understood through several competitive histories rather than a single national-team narrative. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
