    "description": "Wrestling in Iran includes modern international competition and a distinct heritage of athletic ritual and ethical instruction. Understanding the difference between freestyle, Greco-Roman wrestling, and pahlevani traditions helps explain its varied forms.",
    "tags": ["Wrestling", "Freestyle", "Greco-Roman", "Pahlevani", "Olympic sport", "Cultural heritage"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:03:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:03:55Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Wrestling in Iran: Olympic Styles and Pahlevani Traditions",
      "description": "Distinguish Iran's freestyle and Greco-Roman wrestling from pahlevani traditions, with examples from the Paris Olympics and cultural heritage records."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-traditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "One subject, several distinct traditions",
        "content": "An introduction to Iranian wrestling needs to distinguish international competition from the country's traditional athletic culture. United World Wrestling, the international governing body known as UWW, describes freestyle and Greco-Roman as different competitive disciplines. UNESCO separately recognizes pahlevani and zoorkhanei rituals as intangible cultural heritage. These sources describe related areas of athletic life, but they do not establish that their institutions, rules, or training practices are interchangeable. [s1][s2]\n\nIn freestyle, wrestlers can use leg attacks as well as throws and defensive counters. Greco-Roman restricts action to the upper body and prohibits active use of the legs to execute or defend an action. Recognizing the style is therefore essential to understanding what an Iranian competitor is attempting on the mat. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "heritage-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The zoorkhane and the idea of the champion",
        "content": "A zoorkhane is a traditional exercise space associated with pahlevani practice. UNESCO's description brings together physical training, poetry, rhythm, and ethical instruction. A morshed leads the ritual with recitation and drumming, while experienced practitioners transmit expectations of conduct alongside athletic skills. UNESCO inscribed the tradition on its Representative List in 2010. [s2]\n\nThis context broadens the meaning of athletic achievement: strength is presented alongside responsibilities toward other people and the local community. However, the heritage description is evidence about a recognized tradition, not a current survey of all Iranian wrestlers. Its dated facility count should not be repeated as a contemporary national total. Nor does heritage recognition establish an uninterrupted, precisely documented lineage for every modern wrestling practice. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "paris-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Greco-Roman success at Paris",
        "content": "The Paris 2024 Olympics provide a concrete example of Iranian success in Greco-Roman wrestling. Saeid Esmaeili won the 67-kilogram title, joining Mohammadhadi Saravi, the champion at 97 kilograms. UWW's report describes Esmaeili recovering from a deficit against Ukraine's Parviz Nasibov and winning a closely contested final. [s3]\n\nThe same report records Alireza Mohmadi's defeat by Bulgaria's Semen Novikov in the 87-kilogram final. Reading the individual results preserves the distinction between weight classes and avoids turning several different bouts into an undifferentiated claim of national dominance. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "selection-and-renewal",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International success begins with selection",
        "content": "Esmaeili's route to Paris also illustrates the pressure within elite selection. Before the Olympics, UWW reported that he defeated Tokyo Olympic champion Mohammadreza Geraei in a playoff to secure Iran's place at that weight. An established champion's reputation did not automatically determine the next Olympic representative. [s4]\n\nFor a new viewer, the most useful starting questions are therefore practical: which wrestling style is being contested, which weight class is involved, and which tournament or selection stage does the result belong to? These distinctions make the sporting record intelligible while leaving space for the separate cultural history of pahlevani practice. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "styles-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Distinguishing competition styles from cultural practice",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Wrestling and related athletic practice in Iran\"] -->|\"International competition\"| B[\"Freestyle\"]\n  A -->|\"International competition\"| C[\"Greco-Roman\"]\n  A -->|\"Distinct cultural heritage context\"| D[\"Pahlevani and zoorkhanei rituals\"]\n  B -->|\"Permits\"| E[\"Leg attacks and upper-body techniques\"]\n  C -->|\"Restricts action to\"| F[\"Upper-body techniques\"]\n  D -->|\"Combines\"| G[\"Exercise, recitation, rhythm and ethical instruction\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates two international competition styles from a cultural tradition. Its branches classify practices; they do not assert an institutional hierarchy or a proven historical line of descent.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
